---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const ACC = "server/src/iki_server/accounts";
const OAUTH = "server/src/iki_server/oauth";
const SCIM = "server/src/iki_server/scim";
const SETUP = "server/src/iki_server/setup";

const H = {
  short: "Single sign-on for people, OAuth for everything else",
  how: "From an empty install to a signed-in person",
  rules: "Setup, provisioning, linking, clients and sessions",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Iki has no local passwords, ever. People sign in through the organization's identity provider,
      by OpenID Connect or SAML 2.0, and SCIM 2.0 provisions who has access (<Adr n={[23, 26]} />).
      A one-time setup link connects the provider; there is no administrator account before it. A
      person's first sign-in links to the account SCIM made for them by what the provider vouches
      for, strongest first (<Adr n="0151" />). Everything that isn't a browser (the <code>iki</code>{" "}
      CLI, the Python SDK, MCP clients and other apps) gets OAuth tokens from Iki's own OAuth 2.1
      server (<Adr n="0028" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The setup link.</strong> Someone with cluster access runs <code
          >iki-server setup_link</code
        >. The link works once, for 60 minutes, and a new one revokes earlier ones (<Path
          p={`${SETUP}/management/commands/setup_link.py`}
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The wizard.</strong> It names the organization, connects the provider (checking an
        OIDC discovery document, or reading SAML metadata and pinning the signing certificate), and
        asks for a sign-in through it before saving anything. The person who signs in becomes the
        first Org Admin (<Path p={`${SETUP}/views.py`} />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Provisioning.</strong> The provider pushes people and groups to <code>/scim/v2/</code>.
        A SCIM group decides a team's members only once an Org Admin links it to the team (<Adr
          n="0026"
        />, <Path p={`${SCIM}/services.py`} sym="def link_group(" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>First sign-in.</strong> The sign-in is linked to a provisioned account, then keyed by
        the provider's subject identifier from then on (<Path p={`${ACC}/linking.py`} sym="MatchedBy" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Session.</strong> The browser holds a session cookie. Its API calls need CSRF for
        unsafe methods, and only what the person does keeps the session open (<Adr n="0153" />).
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Signing a record signs in again at the same provider; that's on <PageLink
        to="Electronic signatures"
      /> (<Adr n="0124" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="One provider at a time" open>
        <p>
          Exactly one identity provider is enabled. Each issuer gets its own <code>provider_id</code>,
          so accounts from one issuer are never matched to subjects from another. Sign-in runs through
          django-allauth. <code>setup_link --recover</code> runs the same wizard to reconfigure sign-in
          on an installation that's already set up (<Path
            p={`${ACC}/models.py`}
            sym="class IdentityProvider("
          />, <Path p={`${ACC}/oidc.py`} />, <Path p={`${ACC}/saml.py`} />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="How a first sign-in finds its account">
        <p>
          Iki links by anything other than the subject identifier only once: a person's first sign-in
          to the account SCIM provisioned for them, or to a placeholder an imported bundle named. It
          tries, in order:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>the person's immutable ID at the provider, sent as SCIM's <code>externalId</code>;</li>
          <li>the SAML subject, as SCIM's <code>userName</code>;</li>
          <li>an email address the provider says it verified.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The second and third match names, which an organization can give to someone else, so they
          link only active accounts. When two accounts answer to the same rule, nobody is linked (<Adr
            n="0151"
          />, <Path p={`${ACC}/linking.py`} />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="SCIM groups and teams">
        <p>
          A linked team's members are exactly the members of its linked groups; several groups may
          feed one team. A team no group is linked to is managed by hand, and one whose group goes
          away keeps its members. Every SCIM change is audited through the SCIM channel, naming the
          token that made it (<Path p={`${SCIM}/models.py`} sym="class ScimGroup(" />, <Path
            p={`${SCIM}/services.py`}
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="OAuth clients">
        <p>
          Iki is its own OAuth 2.1 authorization server, built on django-oauth-toolkit. Its clients
          are of three kinds: Iki's own CLI and SDK, made by a migration; clients that register
          themselves, by Dynamic Client Registration or a Client ID Metadata Document, which an Org
          Admin approves unless the organization allows any client; and credentials an Org Admin
          makes for a service account (<Adr n={[28, 51]} />, <Path p={`${OAUTH}/models.py`} sym="class Client(" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          People use a client by granting it a consent: some scopes, and all their projects or only
          the ones they pick. What a token may then do is on <PageLink to="Tokens and delegation" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Sessions">
        <p>
          A session ends after an idle timeout (<code>IKI_SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MINUTES</code>, 8 hours
          by default) and, however active, an absolute lifetime (<code>IKI_SESSION_MAX_AGE_HOURS</code>,
          24 hours). Requests a page makes on its own, such as event streams and polls marked <code
            >Iki-Background: 1</code
          >, don't move the idle timeout, so an unattended tab still signs out on time (<Adr
            n="0153"
          />, <Path p={`${ACC}/session_policy.py`} sym='BACKGROUND_HEADER = "Iki-Background"' />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/api/security.py"
            sym="class SessionAuth"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="On the dev cluster">
        <p>
          The dev cluster runs Keycloak with an <code>iki</code> realm, imported on every start. <code
            >make dev-sso</code
          > drives the real setup wizard against it, so dev sign-in goes through the same path as
          production (<Path p="deploy/dev/manifests/keycloak.yaml" />, <Path p="deploy/dev/sso.py" />).
          The rest of the dev cluster is on <PageLink to="The dev cluster" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={ACC} />: sign-in, account linking, sessions, service accounts</li>
        <li><Path p={SCIM} />: SCIM 2.0 provisioning</li>
        <li><Path p={OAUTH} />: the OAuth server, clients and consents</li>
        <li><Path p={SETUP} />: the setup link and wizard</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0023" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0026" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0028" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0151" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0153" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
